      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-12555",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0+4)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =0,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042406/example-12555.json",
          "file_sha256": "48d2ad29f324f3f469da6b4938530a5a0ce829565ac6fb77f85e644feace1e96"
