    {
      "question": "e-5265",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5494d72a6ddaf1056c9587a0e83d3797c7d9ad20689b58228c7b672bc112dc0f",
      "new_sha256": "00c0b01f66f7733525ef909446d686087c5dc2c4dd2e318170555650101ab648",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5265",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4((x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + y^2 + 1 \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is $\\mathbb R^2.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042406/example-5265.json",
          "file_sha256": "93419b7d0aefdc0b3264d829c9fe991641e899a5379b1334ab1e732d21f608b0"
        }
